# Responsive Design
Designs come from Figma (screenshots or handoff) and must match exactly — not just "look responsive."

## Sizing — flutter_screenutil
- use flutter_screenutil for all sizes, fonts, and spacing pulled from a Figma design
- ScreenUtilInit's designSize MUST match the Figma artboard dimensions exactly (check the frame size in Figma before setting this, e.g. Size(375, 812) — never guess it)
- widths/heights from Figma: use .w / .h (e.g. a 24px width in Figma -> 24.w)
- font sizes from Figma: use .sp (e.g. 16.sp)
- radius/corner values: use .r
- never hardcode a raw pixel number from Figma without wrapping it in .w/.h/.sp/.r — a raw number breaks proportional scaling on other devices
- when given a Figma screenshot, extract exact values (spacing, font size, corner radius) rather than approximating — ask for the Figma file/dev-mode inspector values if a screenshot alone leaves a value ambiguous

## Layout structure — LayoutBuilder / breakpoints
flutter_screenutil scales sizes, it does not decide layout structure — that's still LayoutBuilder's job
- breakpoints defined once in lib/core/theme/app_breakpoints.dart (mobile <600, tablet 600-1024, desktop >1024)
- context extension (context.isMobile / isTablet / isDesktop) for screen-level layout decisions
- LayoutBuilder for widget-level layout decisions (column count, side-by-side vs stacked)
- one adaptive layout branch per screen, not scattered conditionals in child widgets

## Rules that still apply regardless of tool
- never disable or override the user's system font scale (textScaler) — accessibility takes priority over exact pixel matching
- test at common breakpoints (360, 600, 768, 1024, 1440) when reviewing generated UI
- if a Figma value and an accessibility requirement conflict (e.g. a font too small to scale), flag it rather than silently picking one
